eft|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{matrix}\\right|\\\\&=\\left|\\left[\\begin{matrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{matrix}\\right]\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\l